CH = dict(num=8, title="Sound Waves: Characteristics and Applications", tag="Physics", theme="Production, propagation, characteristics, echo, ultrasound",
 intro="Sound is energy that reaches us as a wave. This chapter explains how sound is made by vibrations, how it travels through a medium as compressions and rarefactions, what quantities describe a wave (wavelength, frequency, time period, amplitude, speed), how we perceive it (pitch, loudness), and how reflection, echo and ultrasound are put to use.",
 big_questions=["How is sound produced and how does it reach our ears?", "Why can astronauts on a spacewalk not talk directly to each other?", "How do bats find prey in the dark?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "8.1 Production of Sound"),
 ("p", "Every sound comes from something that is **vibrating**. A **vibration** is a periodic to-and-fro motion (oscillation) of an object. The object that makes the sound is the **source** of sound."),
 ("steps", [("Activity — rubber band on an open box", "Pluck a stretched rubber band: you hear a sound as long as the band is visibly vibrating, and the sound stops when the vibration stops. Changing the tension changes the sound. A band held between two fingers also sounds, but much softer, because the box helps the sound to be louder."),
            ("Activity — tuning fork", "Strike a tuning fork on a soft rubber pad (never on a hard surface) and hold it near the ear: sound is heard. Touch a vibrating prong to water: waves splash and ripple, proving that the prongs vibrate.")]),
 ("table", ["Source of sound", "What vibrates", "Example"], [
   ["Stretched string", "The string itself", "Sitar, veena, rubber band"],
   ["Membrane (stretched skin)", "The membrane", "Tabla, drum"],
   ["Air column", "Air inside a hollow pipe", "Bansuri (flute)"],
   ["Metal bar / prongs", "The metal", "Tuning fork, taal (cymbals)"],
   ["Vocal cords (humans)", "Stretched muscular flaps in the voice box (larynx)", "Speech, singing; tongue, lips, mouth and nasal cavity shape it into words"],
   ["Body parts rubbed together", "Wings or legs", "Grasshopper, cricket"]]),
 ("ul", ["In most musical instruments more than one part vibrates.", "A **tuning fork** is a U-shaped steel or aluminium bar with a stem; the two sides of the U are the **prongs (tines)**. It gives an almost single-frequency sound.", "Kongthong (Meghalaya), the 'Whistling Village', gives every person a personal tune name (*Jingrwai Iawbei*), composed by the mother at birth."]),
 ("h2", "8.2 Propagation of Sound"),
 ("p", "Sound travels from the source to our ears through a **medium** — the material through which it propagates. A **vacuum** is a space with no matter in it."),
 ("table", ["Activity", "Observation", "Conclusion"], [
   ["Ear on desk while a friend taps it", "Sound is heard through the desk", "Sound travels through solids"],
   ["Tap two spoons under water", "Tapping is heard through water and air", "Sound travels through liquids"],
   ["Normal listening in a room", "Sound is heard", "Sound travels through gases (air)"],
   ["Electric bell in a bell jar, air pumped out", "Sound fades to almost nothing although the bell is seen ringing; it returns when air is let in", "Sound cannot travel in vacuum — it needs a medium"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "Outer space is a near vacuum, so astronauts on a spacewalk cannot hear each other or the clank of metal directly. They talk through radio devices fitted into their spacesuits. Light, in contrast, does travel through vacuum, which is why sunlight reaches us."),
 ("h2", "8.3 Sound Waves"),
 ("h3", "The slinky analogy"),
 ("p", "Push and pull one end of a slinky: regions where the turns are crowded together and regions where they are spread out travel along its length. A mark made on one turn only **oscillates about its own position, parallel to the disturbance** — it does not travel along. Sound in air behaves in the same way."),
 ("h3", "Compressions and rarefactions"),
 ("p", "Imagine a long tube of air with an oscillating piston at one end. Without oscillation the air has a uniform **average density**."),
 ("ul", ["When the piston moves forward it pushes nearby particles together: a region of **higher than average density** forms, called a **compression (C)**.", "When the piston moves back, a region of **lower than average density** forms, called a **rarefaction (R)**.", "Collisions between neighbouring particles pass each compression and rarefaction forward. A **series of alternating compressions and rarefactions** is a **sound wave**.", "The direction in which the wave moves is the **direction of propagation**."]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "In a sound wave the **disturbance (energy) travels, the particles do not**. Each particle only vibrates about its mean position. A small point source sends spherical waves in all directions."),
 ("h3", "Types of waves"),
 ("table", ["Term", "Meaning", "Example"], [
   ["Longitudinal wave", "Particles vibrate **parallel** to the direction of propagation", "Sound; longitudinal seismic waves (first to be recorded by seismographs)"],
   ["Transverse wave", "Particles vibrate **perpendicular** to the direction of propagation", "Light, waves on a string; some seismic waves"],
   ["Mechanical wave", "Needs a material medium", "Sound (longitudinal), water waves"],
   ["Non-mechanical wave", "Needs no medium", "Light (travels through vacuum)"]]),
 ("p", "Sudden loud sounds (firecrackers, thunder) arise when gases are heated rapidly and expand quickly, sending out a sudden pulse of density change. A **supersonic aircraft**, flying faster than sound, produces a loud **sonic boom**."),
 ("h2", "8.4 Energy of Sound Waves"),
 ("p", "Spread grains of rice over a stretched rubber or cellophane sheet on a wide container and make a loud sound near it (without touching): the sheet vibrates and the grains **jump**. Louder sound makes them jump higher. Hence **sound is a form of energy**, and a sound wave transfers energy from the source through the medium by vibrations and collisions of particles — not by moving the medium itself."),
 ("ul", ["Particles of a medium are never truly at rest (thermal motion); a passing sound wave only temporarily increases their vibration.", "A **microphone** turns sound into an electrical signal (sound makes a thin diaphragm vibrate). A **speaker** does the opposite: an electrical signal makes a cone or diaphragm vibrate and produce sound."]),
 ("h2", "8.5 Graphical Representation of a Sound Wave"),
 ("ul", ["At a given instant, plot **distance on the x-axis** and **density of the medium on the y-axis**; a dashed horizontal line marks the average density.", "Above the line is a **compression**; the highest point is the **crest** (maximum density).", "Below the line is a **rarefaction**; the lowest point is the **trough** (minimum density).", "A graph of density against **time** at one fixed position can also be drawn."]),
 ("h2", "8.6 Characteristics of a Sound Wave"),
 ("table", ["Quantity", "Definition", "Symbol", "SI unit"], [
   ["Wavelength", "Distance between two consecutive crests or two consecutive troughs", "λ", "metre (m)"],
   ["Frequency", "Number of density oscillations per unit time at a fixed point", "ν", "hertz (Hz) = s<sup>−1</sup>"],
   ["Time period", "Time taken for one complete density oscillation at a point", "T", "second (s)"],
   ["Amplitude", "Maximum change in density (in a compression or rarefaction) from the average density", "—", "—"],
   ["Intensity", "Sound energy passing per unit time through a unit area perpendicular to the direction of propagation", "—", "W m<sup>−2</sup>"],
   ["Speed", "Distance a crest (or trough) travels in unit time", "v", "m s<sup>−1</sup>"]]),
 ("note", "FORMULA", "ν = 1 / T &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; and &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; v = λ × ν &nbsp; (speed = wavelength × frequency). A shorter time period means a higher frequency."),
 ("h3", "Frequency and amplitude"),
 ("ul", ["Everyday sounds are a mixture of frequencies; a tuning fork or a careful whistle gives an almost single frequency.", "Musical notes Sa, Re, Ga … each have a distinct frequency; **Sa has the lowest** of the octave and the others rise in order.", "Larger amplitude means more energy; a harder strike makes grains jump higher.", "**Intensity falls with distance** because the same energy spreads over a larger area. A sound of larger initial amplitude travels farther before it dies out."]),
 ("h3", "Speed of sound"),
 ("table", ["Medium (at about 15 °C)", "Approximate speed"], [["Steel (solid)", "5000 m s<sup>−1</sup>"], ["Water (liquid)", "1500 m s<sup>−1</sup>"], ["Air (gas)", "340 m s<sup>−1</sup>"]]),
 ("ul", ["Sound is **fastest in solids, slower in liquids, slowest in gases** (about 4–5 times faster in water and 15–20 times faster in solids than in air).", "In air the speed **rises with temperature and humidity**: about 331 m s<sup>−1</sup> at 0 °C and 344 m s<sup>−1</sup> at 22 °C.", "In most media the speed depends only on the medium, not on the source or the frequency. If the frequency changes, the **wavelength changes and the speed stays constant** (so music of different notes arrives together).", "Lightning is seen before thunder because light is vastly faster; distance to the strike = speed of sound × time delay (5 s → 1.7 km)."]),
 ("h3", "Worked numericals (method)"),
 ("table", ["Problem", "Method", "Answer"], [
   ["10 oscillations in 2 s", "ν = 10/2; T = 1/ν", "5 Hz and 0.2 s"],
   ["Wavelength at 20 Hz and 20 kHz in air (344 m s<sup>−1</sup>)", "λ = v/ν", "17.2 m and 1.72 cm"],
   ["Wave in steel, λ = 50 m, v = 5000 m s<sup>−1</sup>", "ν = v/λ; T = 1/ν", "100 Hz and 0.01 s"],
   ["Clap, echo after 0.5 s (v = 340 m s<sup>−1</sup>)", "d = vt/2 (to and fro)", "85 m"],
   ["Sonar returns after 0.90 s (v = 1530 m s<sup>−1</sup>)", "d = v × (t/2)", "688.5 m"]]),
 ("h2", "8.7 Human Perception of Sound"),
 ("p", "Time period, wavelength, frequency, amplitude and speed are physical and measurable. **Pitch** and **loudness** describe how we *experience* sound and are subjective."),
 ("table", ["Perceived quality", "Linked to", "Details"], [
   ["Pitch", "Frequency", "High frequency → shrill, high pitch (whistle, siren); low frequency → deep, low pitch (thunder, aircraft rumble). The relation is not exactly mathematical."],
   ["Loudness", "Amplitude", "Larger amplitude is heard louder, and it falls as we move away. Intensity can be measured; loudness depends on the listener."],
   ["Timbre (quality)", "Overtones", "Makes the same note on a flute, ektara or tabla sound different; depends on shape, material and construction of the instrument."]]),
 ("ul", ["Voices differ because of frequency and how the throat, mouth and nasal cavity shape sound. In adolescence boys' vocal cords lengthen and thicken, vibrate less often, and the voice deepens.", "**Audible range: 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz (20 kHz)**; it varies by person and shrinks with age. Below 20 Hz is **infrasonic**, above 20 kHz is **ultrasonic**.", "Dogs, cats, bats and dolphins hear ultrasound; elephants hear infrasound.", "Loudness is measured in **decibels (dB)**: rustling leaves a few dB, conversation about 60 dB, firecrackers above 100 dB. A small rise in dB is a large rise in intensity.", "**Noise** is unwanted or harmful sound; long exposure to loud noise harms health, sleep and hearing. Hearing loss is tested with **audiograms** and helped by **hearing aids** (microphone, amplifier, speaker)."]),
 ("h3", "Hearing and music (extra)"),
 ("ul", ["Sound makes the **eardrum** vibrate; tiny bones amplify the vibrations; the **cochlea** converts them into electrical signals for the brain. Two ears let the brain find direction from the tiny time gap (under 1/1000 s).", "A **tone** has a single frequency. A **musical note** combines a lowest **fundamental** frequency with higher **overtones**. An **octave** is the interval in which one note has double the frequency of the other (200 Hz and 400 Hz).", "Sir C. V. Raman (India's first science Nobel, for the Raman Effect) also studied the acoustics of the tabla and mridangam; their central black patch, the **syaahi**, gives rich tonal control."]),
 ("h2", "8.8 Reflection of Sound"),
 ("p", "Sound bounces off solids and liquids and obeys the **same laws of reflection as light**: the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, and the incident ray, reflected ray and normal lie in one plane."),
 ("h3", "Echo"),
 ("ul", ["An **echo** is the sound heard again after reflection from a distant hard surface (cliff, mountain, long corridor).", "The ear can separate two sounds only if they are **at least 0.1 s** apart. In 0.1 s sound covers 340 × 0.1 = 34 m to and fro, so the **minimum distance to the reflector is 17 m**.", "Hard, smooth surfaces give strong echoes; curtains and other soft surfaces absorb sound; rough surfaces scatter it, so no clear echo is heard. In a small room reflections return too soon to be separated."]),
 ("h3", "Reverberation"),
 ("ul", ["Repeated reflections in a large hall make sound **persist after the source stops**. It occurs when reflections arrive less than 0.05 s apart.", "Too much causes garbled sound. Auditoriums are designed for suitable reverberation, using absorbing panels, upholstered seats and curtains.", "Gol Gumbaz at Bijapur has a whispering gallery in which a faint whisper is heard several times across the dome."]),
 ("table", ["Feature", "Echo", "Reverberation"], [["Time gap between sounds", "At least 0.1 s", "Less than 0.05 s"], ["What we hear", "Sound repeated separately", "Original sound prolonged"], ["Where", "Open spaces, cliffs, long corridors", "Large halls and auditoriums"]]),
 ("h2", "8.9 Ultrasonic and Infrasonic Waves and Their Applications"),
 ("table", ["Type", "Frequency", "Uses"], [
   ["Infrasonic", "Below 20 Hz", "Detecting earthquakes, volcanic eruptions and severe storms (they travel far through air and Earth)"],
   ["Audible", "20 Hz – 20 kHz", "Hearing, speech, music"],
   ["Ultrasonic", "Above 20 kHz", "Ultrasonography (imaging organs without surgery); breaking kidney stones; ultrasonic welding; cleaning delicate machine parts; finding defects inside metal blocks; locating objects"]]),
 ("h3", "Echolocation and sonar"),
 ("ul", ["**Echolocation**: bats emit short bursts of ultrasound and use the echoes to sense the position of obstacles and prey in the dark. Dolphins, whales and some birds do the same.", "**Sonar (sound navigation and ranging)**: a ship sends ultrasound into water and analyses the reflected waves to find the distance, direction and speed of submarines, shipwrecks and the sea bed. Depth = speed × (time / 2).", "**Audio surveillance**: low-frequency hum of drones and aircraft can be detected by sensitive sound sensors.", "Frontier: sounds recorded on Mars; timing earthquake sound to measure sea temperature; mosquito buzz used to identify disease carriers; soil microbe crackles to study soil health."]),
 ("h2", "8.10 Quick Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
   ("How is sound produced?", "By vibrating objects, e.g. a plucked string, a struck tuning fork or vocal cords."),
   ("Why can sound not travel in vacuum?", "A sound wave is mechanical; it needs particles to pass on compressions and rarefactions. The bell-jar experiment shows the sound fading as air is removed."),
   ("Why is sound called a longitudinal wave?", "The particles vibrate parallel to the direction in which the wave travels."),
   ("What is the difference between a compression and a rarefaction?", "A compression is a region of higher density than average; a rarefaction is a region of lower density."),
   ("Do particles travel along with a sound wave?", "No. They vibrate about mean positions; only the disturbance and energy travel."),
   ("State the relation between v, λ and ν.", "v = λ × ν; and ν = 1/T."),
   ("Why does sound travel faster in solids than in gases?", "Particles in solids are closer and pass the disturbance quicker; speeds are about 5000 m s<sup>−1</sup> in steel against 340 m s<sup>−1</sup> in air."),
   ("Define pitch and loudness. What do they depend on?", "Pitch is how frequency is perceived (high frequency = shrill); loudness is how amplitude is perceived."),
   ("What is the audible range of humans?", "20 Hz to 20 kHz."),
   ("Why is the minimum distance for hearing an echo 17 m?", "Sounds must be 0.1 s apart; in 0.1 s sound covers 34 m to and fro, so the reflector is 34/2 = 17 m away."),
   ("Differentiate between echo and reverberation.", "Echo is a separate repeated sound after at least 0.1 s; reverberation is a prolonged sound from reflections less than 0.05 s apart."),
   ("How does a bat find its prey in the dark?", "By echolocation: it emits ultrasound and senses the echoes from obstacles and prey."),
   ("In a film a spaceship explodes with a flash and a bang together. What is wrong?", "Sound cannot travel in the vacuum of space, and light would be seen before any sound anyway."),
 ])],
 takeaways=["Sound is produced by **vibrations** and is a **longitudinal mechanical wave** needing a medium; it cannot travel in **vacuum**.", "The **disturbance travels, not the particles**; a wave is a series of **compressions and rarefactions** carrying energy.", "**v = λν** and **ν = 1/T**; speed depends on the medium (**solids > liquids > gases**) and rises with temperature in air.", "**Pitch ↔ frequency, loudness ↔ amplitude, timbre ↔ overtones**; audible range **20 Hz–20 kHz**.", "**Echo**: reflector at least **17 m** away (0.1 s); **reverberation**: gaps under 0.05 s.", "**Ultrasound** is used in sonar, echolocation, imaging and cleaning; **infrasound** helps detect earthquakes and storms."])
